{"availability":"available","full":"docling-project/docling.rs","name":"docling.rs","description":"Full Rust port of Python docling","category":"Other","language":"Rust","stars":93,"forks":23,"created":"2026-06-27T16:51:49Z","updated":"2026-10-03T16:08:43Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":[],"demo":"https://docling-project.github.io/docling.rs/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.843Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.843Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.843Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.843Z","stars":93,"forks":23}],"technologies":["Rust"],"lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.843Z","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A Rust port of docling: convert documents into a unified DoclingDocument for downstream AI workflows.","Fast and small: one static binary, no Python/PyTorch at runtime. The PDF ML pipeline runs 4.3× faster than Python docling at 2.3–2.6× less peak RAM (methodology, profiling and per-fixture numbers in docs/PDFCONFORMANCE.md § Performance); declarative formats (DOCX/HTML/XLSX/…) convert 20–60× faster at 60× less memory (measured with scripts/test/performance.sh, Python docling vs Rust on the same file). Output is checked against upstream for every format — see Conformance with Python docling.","▶ Try it in your browser — the whole converter compiled to wasm: drop a DOCX, PDF, XLSX, EPUB … and get Markdown, docling JSON, DocLang XML, LaTeX or a Pandoc AST back. Nothing is uploaded; the page runs entirely on your device, phone included. Scanned pages can be OCR'd there too (layout + PP-OCR + TableFormer via ONNX Runtime Web) once you point it at the models. See crates/docling-wasm."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/docling-project/docling.rs/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"ff3b444a3d8ed29afe5673e04c9f480abf7ecfb4142bc99cff068d31d18feb16","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T16:22:56.364Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:39.943Z","overv